AI-Based Drowsiness Detection System
Part 3 (Questions 41–60)
Q41. What is a .task model? Why did you use it instead of a .tflite model?
Answer

A .task file is a packaged model format used by the newer MediaPipe Tasks API. It contains more than just the neural network weights.

Typically, a .task file includes:

The trained TensorFlow Lite model
Model metadata
Input and output specifications
Configuration information required by the Tasks API

This makes it easier to load and use because the API already knows how to interpret the model.

A .tflite file contains only the TensorFlow Lite model itself. When using it directly, developers often need to manually handle preprocessing, postprocessing, and input/output tensors.

I used the .task model because the MediaPipe Face Landmarker API is designed to work with it directly, making development simpler and reducing configuration errors.

Follow-up

Can you convert a .tflite model into a .task model?

Yes. Google provides tools to package TensorFlow Lite models with metadata into .task files.

Q42. What is the difference between face detection and face landmark detection?
Answer

Face detection answers only one question:

"Where is the face?"

The output is usually a bounding box around the face.

Face landmark detection goes a step further.

It answers:

"Where are the important points on the face?"

Instead of just drawing a rectangle, it predicts hundreds of key points such as:

Eye corners
Eyelids
Nose tip
Lips
Jawline
Eyebrows

My project requires landmark detection because I need precise eye geometry rather than just the location of the face.

Interview Tip

Many candidates confuse these two concepts. A face detector cannot directly determine whether the eyes are open or closed.

Q43. Why does MediaPipe track the face instead of detecting it in every frame?
Answer

Running a face detector on every frame would be computationally expensive.

MediaPipe first detects the face and then tracks it across subsequent frames.

Tracking is much cheaper than running the detector repeatedly.

Only when tracking confidence decreases significantly or the face disappears does MediaPipe perform a fresh face detection.

This approach:

Improves FPS
Reduces CPU usage
Produces smoother landmark movement
Avoids unnecessary computation
Follow-up

What happens if the face completely leaves the camera view?

Tracking fails. MediaPipe then runs the face detector again to locate the face once it reappears.

Q44. What is confidence score in MediaPipe?
Answer

A confidence score represents how certain the model is about its prediction.

For example:

0.98 → Very confident
0.75 → Moderately confident
0.20 → Low confidence

MediaPipe allows developers to specify minimum confidence thresholds.

If the prediction confidence falls below the threshold, the result can be ignored to prevent unreliable detections.

Using confidence thresholds improves overall robustness.

Follow-up

What happens if the confidence threshold is set to 0.99?

Very few detections may pass the threshold, causing missed detections.

Q45. What happens if the confidence threshold is too low?
Answer

A very low threshold accepts weak predictions.

Advantages:

Detects more faces.

Disadvantages:

Increased false positives
Less stable landmarks
More noisy detections

Therefore, confidence thresholds should be carefully chosen to balance sensitivity and accuracy.

Q46. Does your project support multiple faces?
Answer

My current implementation is designed primarily for monitoring one user.

Although MediaPipe can detect multiple faces, the application focuses on the first detected face because the objective is to monitor the person using the computer.

Supporting multiple faces would require:

Tracking each face separately
Maintaining independent timers
Managing multiple alarm states

This would significantly increase application complexity.

Follow-up

How would you support multiple users?

I would assign a unique ID to each detected face and maintain separate eye-state timers and alarm logic for each one.

Q47. Why did you use threads in your desktop version?
Answer

The desktop application has multiple independent tasks:

Webcam processing
GUI updates
System tray operations
User interactions

If everything runs on a single thread, a slow operation could freeze the interface.

By moving the detection loop into a separate thread:

The GUI remains responsive.
The webcam continues processing.
The system tray stays interactive.
The application feels smooth.

This separation improves responsiveness and user experience.

Follow-up

What problems can occur with multithreading?

Shared data access can lead to race conditions, deadlocks, or inconsistent state if not synchronised properly.

Q48. Why shouldn't heavy AI inference run on the GUI thread?
Answer

GUI frameworks use an event loop to respond to user actions such as button clicks and window repainting.

If heavy AI inference runs on the same thread, the event loop becomes blocked.

The user experiences:

Frozen windows
Delayed button responses
"Not Responding" messages

Running inference on a background thread allows the GUI thread to continue processing events while detection happens independently.

Q49. Why did you create separate classes like SleepDetector, SleepDetectionGUI, and SleepDetectionTray?
Answer

This follows the principle of Separation of Concerns.

Each class has a single responsibility:

SleepDetector handles camera capture, MediaPipe inference, and drowsiness detection.
SleepDetectionGUI manages the graphical user interface.
SleepDetectionTray manages the system tray icon and user interactions from the tray.

This design provides several benefits:

Easier debugging
Better readability
Improved maintainability
Easier testing
Better scalability

If all functionality were placed inside one file, the code would become difficult to maintain as the project grows.

Follow-up

What software engineering principle does this follow?

The Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) from the SOLID principles.

Q50. How do you safely close your application?
Answer

A proper shutdown involves cleaning up all resources.

When the application exits:

Stop the detection thread.
Release the webcam using cap.release().
Stop the alarm if it is playing.
Quit the Pygame mixer.
Destroy all OpenCV windows.
Close the GUI gracefully.
Remove the system tray icon if necessary.

This prevents memory leaks, locked webcam devices, and orphaned background threads.

Q51. If you wanted to store drowsiness events, how would you design the backend?
Answer

I would expose a REST API from a Node.js/Express backend.

Whenever drowsiness is detected, the desktop application would send a POST request containing:

User ID
Timestamp
Duration of eye closure
Device information (optional)

The backend would validate the request and store the event in MongoDB.

This would allow historical analysis, reports, and dashboards without changing the detection logic.

Follow-up

Would you store video frames in MongoDB?

No. Video files are large and inefficient to store directly in MongoDB. I would store metadata in MongoDB and save videos in object storage or a file server if needed.

Q52. If you had to design the MongoDB schema, what would it look like?
Answer

A simple schema could include:

{
  userId,
  timestamp,
  eyeClosureDuration,
  alertTriggered,
  deviceName,
  createdAt
}

This schema captures the essential information needed for reporting and analysis.

Indexes on userId and timestamp would improve query performance when retrieving a user's history.

Q53. How would you expose this functionality as an API?
Answer

I would design REST endpoints such as:

POST /api/drowsiness → Store a new detection event.
GET /api/drowsiness/:userId → Retrieve a user's history.
GET /api/stats → Return aggregated statistics.
DELETE /api/drowsiness/:id → Remove an event if required.

This keeps the backend modular and allows future integration with web or mobile applications.

Q54. How would you secure the API?
Answer

Several security measures should be implemented:

JWT authentication to verify users.
HTTPS for encrypted communication.
Input validation to prevent malformed requests.
Rate limiting to reduce abuse.
Authentication middleware for protected routes.
Proper error handling without exposing internal details.

These practices help protect both the API and stored data.

Q55. How would you deploy this complete system?
Answer

The architecture would consist of:

Desktop application running locally for detection.
Backend hosted on a cloud platform such as Render, Railway, or AWS.
MongoDB Atlas as the cloud database.
React dashboard hosted on Vercel or Netlify.

The desktop application would communicate securely with the backend over HTTPS.

This separates real-time processing from data storage and reporting.

Q56. Why didn't you train your own deep learning model?
Answer

Training a custom model would require:

A large labelled dataset
Significant computational resources
Time for experimentation and evaluation

My goal was to build a complete, production-style application rather than focus on model research.

MediaPipe already provides highly optimised and well-tested models for facial landmark detection, allowing me to concentrate on application design, integration, and real-time performance.

Follow-up

Would training your own model improve accuracy?

Potentially, but only if trained on a high-quality, task-specific dataset. A poorly trained custom model could perform worse than MediaPipe.

